<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shams al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1021</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shams al-Dawla was the Buyid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> from 997 to about 1021, a son of Fakhr al-Dawla. He governed at first alongside his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> at Ray, then independently, and fought the Kurdish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayhids">Hasanwayhids</a>. The philosopher and physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> served as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and physician.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Shams al-Dawla" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Shams_al-Dawla,_the_Buyid_ruler_of_Hamadan_and_Qirmisin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shams al-Dawla, whose own name was Abu Taher and whose regnal name means Sun of the State, was a son of the Buyid ruler Fakhr al-Dawla. When his father died in 997, his elder brother Abu Taleb Rostam, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a>, took power in the bulk of their father's possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>, while Abu Taher received the governorships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and Kirmanshah. Since both sons were still minors, their mother, known as the Sayyida, assumed the regency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Both brothers at first took the title of Shâhanshâh, implying that they were subordinate to no one. They abandoned it when they accepted the authority of their cousin Baha' al-Dawla, by 1009 or 1010 at the latest. In 1006 or 1007 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> tried to throw off his mother's regency. The Sayyida gained the support of the Kurdish ruler Badr ibn Hasanwaih and of Shams al-Dawla, whose forces laid siege to Ray and fought several battles with Majd al-Dawla's troops.</p><p>When Ray was finally taken, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> was imprisoned for a year, and Shams al-Dawla ruled the city during that time; when the Sayyida released his brother, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>. Around 1013, after the death of Badr ibn Hasanwaih, Shams al-Dawla occupied part of Badr's territory. Later in his reign he tried to replace Majd al-Dawla as ruler of Ray, but the Sayyida foiled his plans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1015 Shams al-Dawla called upon the prominent Persian scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> to treat him. During Avicenna's stay at his court Shams al-Dawla made him his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, so that the physician who attended the ruler also served as his chief minister. Shams al-Dawla died in 1021, having ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> since 997, and was succeeded by his son Sama' al-Dawla.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shams al-Dawla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibn Sina served the Buyid emir <b>Shams al-Dawla</b> of Hamadan as vizier, and al-Sahib ibn Abbad gathered scholars, including the Mu'tazili theologian Abd al-Jabbar, at Ray.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shams al-Dawla</b>, the Buyid ruler of Hamadan, and the Kakuyid dynasty soon took over his territories, and the Hasanwayhids disappeared by 1015, partly replaced by the Annazid dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasanwayhid-dynasty">Hasanwayhid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.8,48.52&amp;year=1011" aria-label="Hamadan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Shams al-Dawla"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4199 4550h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4199 4550h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.8,48.52&amp;year=1011">What was here in 1011? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
